\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards has become the standard post-training stage for autoregressive (AR) language models \citep{ouyang2022instructgpt,shao2024deepseekmath,deepseek2025r1}. The estimator behind it is the score-function policy gradient: sample a completion, compare its reward to a group baseline, and push the log-probability of every token up or down \citep{williams1992reinforce,schulman2017ppo}. A new family of language models does not sample tokens at all. Continuous-state language models such as ELF \citep{hu2026elf} run flow matching \citep{lipman2022flow,liu2022rectified} in a frozen embedding space and only discretise at the final step; their generation is a chain of Gaussian transitions over a $L\times d$ real-valued state, and the whole chain is differentiable. The natural question is whether AR's estimator should be ported to them, as has been done for image flows \citep{black2023ddpo,liu2025flowgrpo,xue2025dancegrpo} and for masked diffusion language models \citep{zhao2025d1,zhu2025llada15}.

Our answer is no, for a structural reason (\S\ref{sec:estimators}). The score of a Gaussian transition is white noise times a Jacobian, $\nabla_\theta\log\mathcal N(y_k)=\varepsilon_k\sigma_k^{-1}\alpha_k\,\partial D_\theta/\partial\theta$, and a trajectory carries $10^{5}$--$10^{6}$ such coordinates, none of which individually decides a token because token commitment is an emergent property of the accumulated dynamics. A reward bit is therefore averaged over five to six orders of magnitude more nuisance directions than in the AR case, where it is attached to $\sim$30 categorical decisions. No refinement of the advantage changes that ratio: with oracle-exact advantages, per-step per-position credit, per-position PPO ratios with ideal clip statistics, or advantages selected by a live critic, every score-function variant we ran on Countdown caps at $+3$--$+5$ points, while token-level GRPO/PPO lifts a GPT-2 trained on the same data by $+9.7$.

The pathwise estimator, which is absent from AR post-training only because categorical sampling has no reparameterisation, is the native one here: $\nabla_\theta V_\phi(y_k)=\partial V_\phi/\partial y_k\cdot\alpha_k\,\partial D_\theta/\partial\theta$ delivers a deterministic signal to every coordinate, provided a critic $V_\phi$ can score a noisy intermediate state. This is the regime of continuous control and of reward back-propagation for image diffusion \citep{lillicrap2015ddpg,haarnoja2018sac,prabhudesai2023alignprop,clark2023draft,domingoenrich2024adjoint}, whose one known failure mode is a critic that the policy learns to exploit. We therefore make three design decisions and test each: the critic is \hl{per-token (2D)}, scoring every position of the noisy state and pooling to a value, so that gradient reaches every position rather than a single pooled bottleneck; it is \hl{trained jointly} with the policy on the policy's own rollouts (a frozen critic is exploited within a few hundred updates); and it is trained and applied \hl{under the deployment sampler}, because gains lock to the sampler they were trained under. Each update takes one random denoising step, which keeps memory constant in the chain length. We call the recipe \jpc{}.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
(i) A variance argument for why score-function RL is capped on continuous-state language models and why pathwise gradients are their native estimator (\S\ref{sec:estimators}), with a table of exhausted score-function variants and an AR control that isolates the architecture.
(ii) \jpc{}, whose central object is the jointly trained 2D critic (\S\ref{sec:method}); the scalar critic of prior reward-backpropagation work is recovered as its pool-then-head special case, and we show the ordering between the two is a learning-rate effect.
(iii) Evidence on four verifiable tasks with three seeds each (\S\ref{sec:experiments}): \jpc{} is the only recipe stable across every task and seed, with $+13.7$ points on a set-split Game24 where more supervised fine-tuning \emph{hurts}, GRPO gives $+2.1$, and an AR model with token-level PPO gives $+5.3$.
(iv) An amortisation loop (\S\ref{sec:loop}) that fine-tunes the model on its own verified samples: it lifts cheap samplers by up to $+17$ points, and two controls (the same loop from the SFT base; gold derivations for the same problems) attribute the gain to the RL policy.
We also report where the recipe does not help (\S\ref{sec:ablations}): the loop is null on Sudoku, where solutions are unique and cheap samplers already match the deployment one, and on Knights-and-Knaves it is driven by problem coverage rather than by RL.
